Linux command line

Shell is a program that takes keyboard input
and passes it to the OS.

Shell prompt usually follows username@machinename
format.

date - prints current date.
cal - prints the calendar.
df - prints the current available space.
free - print currently free RAM.
pwd - print working directory.
ls - list files.
cd - change current directory, either using relative
or absolute path.

ls - list files; also can list mult. dirs.
ls -lt - long format plus sorted by time.

mkdir - create directory.
cp - copy file or a directory.
mv - move and rename.
rm - remove.
Better test the rm with echo or ls if using
globbing.

type - defines command's type.
which - determine location.
help & --help - display usage info.
man - display manual pages:
1. User commands
2. PI for kernel syscalls
3. PI for C libs
4. Special files such as device nodes and drivers
5. File formats
6. Games
7. Misc.
8. Sysadmin commands

apropos - display appropriate command.
whatis - display one-line manual page.
info - display command info entry.

/usr/share/doc - useful documentation for software.

alias - shortcut for defined series of commands.
